      'GTAO (Jimenez et al. 2016, structured after Intel\'s XeGTAO) treats the depth buffer as a height field. For each pixel it picks a few slices, screen-space lines through the pixel, and walks both ways along each to find the highest horizon on either side, with samples bunched near the pixel where contact occlusion lives and distant occluders fading out over the last part of the radius. The two horizons are clamped to the hemisphere around the normal, and the cosine-weighted visible arc between them has a closed form, so each slice is exact for what it sees; averaging slices over many directions converges to ray-traced AO of the height field. Classic SSAO, the comparison, scatters points in a hemisphere and counts the ones that land behind the depth buffer: it needs a bias against self-occlusion and a range check against far-away occluders, and it is noisier for the same number of reads.',
      'The same integral, weighted by direction, gives the bent normal: the average unoccluded direction. Diffuse light is looked up along it, so a crease is lit from where its opening is rather than from straight out of the surface ("Bent vs normal" shows how far it leans). Visibility then goes through Jimenez\'s multi-bounce fit (bright surfaces lose less light in creases, because it bounces back out) and darkens reflections through Lagarde\'s specular occlusion.',
      'Noise is the price of few reads, and three passes pay it off. Each frame turns the slices and shifts the steps; a temporal pass reprojects last frame\'s AO through the depth buffer and keeps a running average per pixel, dropping history wherever last frame\'s depth at that spot belongs to another surface; then a depth- and normal-aware blur smooths what is left. TAA is not used for this: its colour clamp is built for edges, and against per-pixel AO noise it either keeps the noise or ghosts. TAA still runs on the final image for anti-aliasing. Turn both denoisers off to see one frame\'s raw result; "History length" shows where accumulation just restarted (red), for instance along edges while the camera drifts.',
